NNAAMMEE
     veldxf - convert a DXF drawing to vellum format

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     vveellddxxff [-llaayyeerr _n_a_m_e=_n] [-ssccaallee _N] _f_i_l_e._d_x_f > _f_i_l_e._d

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     vveellddxxff reads a DXF file (the R10-era plain-text format the
     office CAD writes) and prints an ordinary vveelllluumm drawing on
     standard output: nothing drawn elsewhere is locked out.
     Import is a CONVERSION, not a link - run it once, and the
     result is a drawing like any other; nothing remembers where
     it came from.

     The scope is the honest entity subset:

                        LINEa vellum L line
                        CIRCLEa C circle
                        ARCan A arc (centre, radius and angles land exactly)
                        TEXTa T label at the nearest of the three sizes
                        POLYLINEa P polyline (LWPOLYLINE too; split at the
                                  20-point ceiling)

     Everything else is SKIPPED AND COUNTED: the run ends with a
     report like ``dropped: 12 SPLINE, 3 INSERT'' on the diagnos-
     tic output, because a silent partial import is how drawings
     lose limbs.  Flatten blocks at the source; a SPLINE can be
     chorded by whatever exported it.

     DXF layer names fold onto vellum's four layers through the
     -llaayyeerr map; a bare digit name (``0''..``3'') folds onto it-
     self and anything unmapped lands on layer 0.  -ssccaallee gives
     the number of drawing units in one grid unit - the default 1
     takes one unit as one millimetre - and the matching UU units
     header is emitted.  Coordinates may carry decimals; they are
     handled in fixed point (no floating point anywhere).  The
     drawing's minimum corner lands at grid 0,0 with the y axis
     flipped (DXF y runs up), so the vveelllluumm -ddxxff export and this
     import FIX-POINT: a converted drawing re-exports and re-im-
     ports byte-identically.

